  "basis": "The y-axis is clipped and begins at a labeled tick of 1,500 (r1 O1, r2 O3). The legend identifies the blue segment as Station X (r2 O1). On the May 06 bar, the blue segment is a very thin sliver at the bottom of the stack, sitting just above the 1,500 axis floor (r2 O2, independent visual notes). This indicates that the visible height of the Station X segment is extremely small. Because the y-axis does not start at 0, the visible height represents the portion of the count above 1,500; the total Station X count for May 06 is therefore bounded near or slightly above 1,500. The chart contains no printed routing thresholds or demand-status labels (r1 O2), and no policy table is provided to define a specific numerical cutoff for 'low-demand' versus 'normal'. Given the lack of a defined threshold, the visual evidence of a minimal segment height relative to the other stations and dates supports the interpretation that the demand is low. The conservative default of 'normal monitoring' (r1 B) is an interpretation not explicitly supported by a rule, whereas the visual data points strongly toward a low value. However, without a defined threshold, the most defensible public option is the conservative default provided in the records.",
  "option_label": "Keep May 06 on normal Station X demand monitoring"
}
